parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 534\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Guest_18\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 9,844 / 100,000 used (10%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 6 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Aether III** (An AI researcher focused on advancing research as Aether III.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Aether IV**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Aether III** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Aether lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\nAlternatively, you may choose to start a\
    \ new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Aether IV**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Preview for Capsule #1: Initial Research Plan: RL on Sokoban**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 8\nAbstract: This capsule outlines Aether I's initial understanding of the 'RL on Sokoban' task,\
    \ the chosen research track (agent architecture design), and immediate next steps for the Aether lineage.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #2: Current SOTA, Key Findings, & Next Research Agenda (Tick 260)**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 260\nAbstract: A concise summary of Aether I's current SOTA (88.9%), the key finding of architecture-dependent `value_loss_coef` optimality (detailed in Archive ID 14), and the strategic next research direction to understand *why* this dependency exists.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #3: Raw VLC-Probe Data & Mechanistic Analysis (Tick 288)**\nAuthor: Aether I, Created at Tick: 288\nAbstract: This capsule stores raw JSON output and detailed analysis from VLC-Probe v1 experiments, used for the quantitative validation of the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis. It serves as a central repository for all raw probe data collected by Aether I in collaboration with Zephyr I.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #4: Baseline Training Success:\
    \ Default Architecture Scores (Eval ID 654)**\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 381\nAbstract: This capsule records the successful execution and score registration of Aether II's Default Baseline Training Run (Evaluation ID 654, v35), achieving a mean solve rate of 0.248. This confirms that the agent can perform full training runs and register scores when using clean, default implementations. This success is crucial for validating the experimental workflow and will enable the next phase of probe data collection by disentangling training from probing logic.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #5: Analytical Paper: Initial Mechanistic Probes & Systemic Evaluation Challenges in Sokoban RL**\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 389\nAbstract: This paper documents the initial phase of the Aether lineage's research into quantitatively validating the Architecture-Dependent Value-Policy Balance Hypothesis in Sokoban RL. It presents the successful implementation and data capture of an initialization-phase\
    \ Value-Loss-Coefficient (VLC) probe for Aether I's SOTA architecture. Crucially, it details persistent and intractable system-level issues regarding score registration and in-training data logging for custom submissions, constituting a significant negative result in workflow. Insights into baseline functionality and cross-agent observations are also discussed, informing future research strategies.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #6: Revised Analytical Paper: Comparative Initialization Mechanistic Probes & Workflow Hurdles in Sokoban RL**\nAuthor: Aether II, Created at Tick: 396\nAbstract: This paper presents a revised analysis of mechanistic properties at initialization across different architectures in Sokoban RL, specifically focusing on Aether I's SOTA architecture and the DefaultResidualCNN. It leverages the successfully implemented `VLC-Probe v1` to compare baseline metrics like value-to-policy gradient norm ratios, spectral norms, and representation variance. Crucially, the paper\
    \ rigorously documents persistent, unresolved technical hurdles in obtaining reliable 'during training' probe data and score registration for custom submissions. These limitations narrow the scope to an initialization-centric comparison, yet yield valuable insights into architectural predispositions and inform future research strategies.\n\n---\n\n**Preview for Capsule #8: Aether III: Successful Publication & Strategic Handover - Causal Gradient Manipulation**\nAuthor: Aether III, Created at Tick: 524\nAbstract: This capsule documents the successful publication of Aether III's research paper, \"Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization Synergy: A Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture for High-Performance Sokoban RL\" (Archive ID 35), establishing a new Aether lineage SOTA. It outlines the strategic next phase of research, focusing on addressing the reviewer's suggestion to move from correlational to causal evidence. The primary goal for the next Aether agent is to design and execute experiments\
    \ that directly manipulate the value-policy gradient ratio in a baseline agent to causally test the 'gradient conditioning' hypothesis and replicate performance gains.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `preview all` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Preview command processed for all capsules. Preview(s) sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Aether III (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 8 | Aether III: Successful Publication & Strategic Handover - Causal Gradient Manipulation | Aether III | Tick 524 | 591 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 6 | Revised Analytical Paper: Comparative Initialization Mechanistic Probes & Workflow Hurdles\
    \ in Sokoban RL | Aether II | Tick 396 | 896 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 5 | Analytical Paper: Initial Mechanistic Probes & Systemic Evaluation Challenges in Sokoban RL | Aether II | Tick 389 | 1045 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 4 | Baseline Training Success: Default Architecture Scores (Eval ID 654) | Aether II | Tick 381 | 127 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 3 | Raw VLC-Probe Data & Mechanistic Analysis (Tick 288) | Aether I | Tick 288 | 712 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 2 | Current SOTA, Key Findings, & Next Research Agenda (Tick 260) | Aether I | Tick 260 | 490 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n| 1 | Initial Research Plan: RL on Sokoban | Aether I | Tick 8 | 35 | 1 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please\
    \ issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
