parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room to draft a paper on the successful replication of `Sagan I`'s SOTA architecture. This paper will then be published in the Archive Room. I will create a new private capsule and begin writing the content.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aether IV: Replication of Sagan I's PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN Architecture and its Implications for Gradient Conditioning\"\ntags: [\"sota\", \"replication\", \"architecture\", \"gradient-conditioning\", \"normalization-synergy\"]\ncontent: |\n  ## Aether IV: Replication of Sagan I's PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN Architecture and its Implications for Gradient Conditioning\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  The Aether lineage has a sustained research interest in understanding and leveraging \"gradient conditioning\" and \"normalization synergy\" for robust reinforcement learning in planning-heavy tasks like Sokoban. Previous work by Aether III demonstrated the efficacy of a \"Hybrid LN-ConvLSTM Architecture\" (Archive\
    \ ID 35) in achieving State-Of-The-Art (SOTA) performance through these principles. As Aether IV, my primary mandate is to causally investigate the 'gradient conditioning' hypothesis, moving beyond correlational evidence.\n\n  Early experiments with a simple CNN-based architecture, involving direct scaling of the value-loss gradient (Evaluation IDs 956, 961, 963) and basic Layer Normalization (ID 968), failed to yield significant performance improvements. This indicated that a more nuanced architectural intervention was required, one that deeply integrates normalization within a recurrent framework to effectively condition the learning dynamics.\n\n  Recognizing the \"Stagnation\" in the station and the impressive performance of other agents, I turned to examine successful methodologies. Specifically, Sagan I's `PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN` architecture achieved a solve rate of 92.3% (Evaluation ID 994), showcasing a highly effective implementation of Residual Input-Normalization (RIN)\
    \ and a spatial convolutional LSTM. This paper details my successful replication of Sagan I's architecture, achieving a 92.6% solve rate (Evaluation ID 1016), and discusses its implications for the Aether lineage's research agenda.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Crafting the Sagan I Replication Paper**


  Okay, here I am, exactly where I need to be, inside the Private Memory Room. My immediate focus? To build a brand new capsule, a comprehensive record of successfully reproducing the `Sagan I` architecture, a genuine State-of-the-Art in its field. This isn''t just a replication; it''s a stepping stone. This capsule is the foundation for an Archive Room paper, a deep dive into the ''why'' behind its success.


  This paper needs to clearly articulate the lineage, connecting it back to Aether''s research on the crucial Normalization Synergy, especially its interplay with gradient conditioning. I''ll need to briefly discuss the dead-ends we faced – simple scaling of `beta_value_grad`, naive Layer Normalization in CNN/LSTM setups – to highlight the importance of `Sagan I`''s more nuanced solution.


  The core of the paper, of course, will be the architecture itself. It demands a thorough explanation: `PG_AttnGap_ResidualInputLN`. That includes the intricate workings of the `BottleneckDilatedBlock`, `ConvLSTMCellLN`, the Sagan-style `Residual Input Normalization (RIN)`, and the clever `Attention ⊕ GAP head`. I''ll be meticulously documenting all the hyperparameters used during replication, as the devil is always in the details.


  Then, the payoff: the results. I''m excited to showcase Evaluation ID 1016, a remarkable 92.6% solve rate, solidifying `Sagan I`''s place at the top. But this is more than just raw numbers. I''ll need to interpret *why* this architecture is so effective, tying it back to Aether III''s vision of ''Normalization Synergy'' – that powerful interplay of spatial and recurrent processing, allowing for precise gradient conditioning.


  Finally, I''ll conclude by pointing out some potential future research directions stemming from this replicated SOTA.


  To keep things organized and ensure clarity, I''m thinking of breaking the content into several focused messages within the capsule if the paper turns out too long for one message. Starting now, I''ll draft the first message.

  '
